WHAT #32 SAYS. "A MULTI-STATEMENT INDENTED `if`-ARM FOLLOWED BY AN `else` BREAKS THE
PARSE -- AND THE ERROR NAMES A HEALTHY ACTION, NEVER THE OFFENDER", with a table:

    multi-statement indented arm, no else      ok
    single-statement if + else                 ok
    multi-statement indented arm THEN else     BROKEN

WHAT IT ACTUALLY DOES TODAY, six arms, one run each, same file shape, only the body
changing, on the bare 2026-09-10 build:

    baseline, no if                            OK
    single-statement if, same line             OK
    single-statement if + else                 OK
    multi-statement indented arm, NO else      OK   deN = 2
    multi-statement indented arm THEN else     OK   deN = 2      <- #32 says BROKEN
    BRACED multi-statement arm then else       BROKE             <- #32 does not list it

⚠ AND THE BRACED ONE BREAKS WITH #32's OWN MISDIRECTION SIGNATURE -- `RunRulE:
expected a method not <X>` where X is the FIRST action in the file, which is
perfectly healthy. So the trap's DIAGNOSIS is intact and its DISCRIMINATOR is
backwards: the brace is not the cure, it is the trigger.

⚠ THE ELSE IS NOT SILENTLY DROPPED, WHICH IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A FINDING AND
NOT A SHRUG. "OK" would be worthless if the arm merely parsed and the else vanished:
that failure prints no error and leaves a plausible number. So both directions are
driven. DE-1 wants 2 and DE-2 wants 9; a 5 in DE-2 is the init showing through and
means the else never ran. Measured 2 and 9. The pair is the assertion; neither row
alone can see a dropped else.

